# infra/backup/.env — layer 2 (restic) configuration. Gitignored.
#
# This file is read TWO ways: sourced by infra/backup/run.sh, and parsed by
# systemd as an EnvironmentFile for restic-check.service. Keep every line plain
# KEY=value — no `export`, no quoting, no shell expansion, no command
# substitution. systemd does not run a shell over it and will not tell you it
# skipped a line.
#
# The shell reader is the sharper edge, because it does not merely skip a line
# it dislikes — it RUNS it. Three characters that arrive by copy-paste and are
# not safe here:
#
#   &  ends the command. `X=a&b=c` backgrounds the assignment in a subshell,
#      so X never reaches the parent shell and ends up EMPTY, silently.
#   ?  is where a URL's query string starts, and a query string is where the &
#      lives. Strip from the `?` onward — see KUMA_PUSH_URL below.
#   #  after a space starts a comment for the shell and does NOT for systemd,
#      so the two readers would disagree about the value.
#
# Nothing here needs any of them.

# The restic repository: SFTP against the Proxmox host's existing sshd. /restic
# is inside the `resticbackup` user's chroot, on the usbbackup pool. Setting it
# up is Part 1 of docs/backup-setup.md and happens on the host. The account is
# NOT called `backup` — Debian ships a stock system account by that name — and
# is not named after a machine, because every client shares it.
RESTIC_REPOSITORY=sftp:resticbackup@pve.thefipster.de:/restic

# THE ONE SECRET THAT CANNOT BE IN THE BACKUP. Lose it and the repository is
# cryptographically gone — there is no recovery, no support, no reset.
#
# The authoritative copy belongs OUTSIDE the lab: on paper, or in an account
# that survives the building. Keep a copy in Vaultwarden by all means, but that
# is the convenient one, not the authoritative one — the vault is inside this
# backup, so storing the key only there closes a circle where losing the infra
# VM loses both at once.
RESTIC_PASSWORD=

# Uptime Kuma push monitor. Pinged ONLY by a fully clean run, so silence is the
# signal. Its row is in docs/uptime-kuma-monitors.md. Leave empty to disable the
# heartbeat — the backup still runs, you just stop being told when it doesn't.
#
# TOKEN ONLY — STRIP THE QUERY STRING. Kuma displays the URL with one already
# attached:
#
#   https://uptime.thefipster.de/api/push/TOKEN?status=up&msg=OK&ping=
#                                             ^ delete from here to the end
#
# That `&` ends the line as a command separator when run.sh sources this file
# as shell: the assignment runs in a background subshell, never reaches the
# parent, and the push is silently skipped — backups keep succeeding while the
# monitor sits red forever, with nothing printed either way. run.sh builds the
# query itself with `curl --get --data-urlencode`, so everything from the `?`
# is redundant as well as dangerous. Replace `changeme` with the token and
# nothing else:
KUMA_PUSH_URL=https://uptime.thefipster.de/api/push/changeme
